WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:01.110
Hank: Good morning, John.

00:00:01.110 --> 00:00:04.340
I live in a small town in Montana, where I
have several times been asked if I am interested

00:00:04.340 --> 00:00:05.870
in running for local election.

00:00:05.870 --> 00:00:10.320
As a local business owner and a semi-celebrity
and a person who is very good at picking karaoke

00:00:10.320 --> 00:00:11.969
songs, I would have an okay chance of winning.

00:00:11.969 --> 00:00:15.240
But what pops into my mind every time someone
asks me this question is that I'm not really

00:00:15.240 --> 00:00:17.600
from Missoula, Montana, I'm from the internet.

00:00:17.600 --> 00:00:22.240
Now that raises some interesting questions
about like, the concept of place and the future

00:00:22.240 --> 00:00:26.770
of local government and the future of the
autonomy of the internet, but those are big,

00:00:26.770 --> 00:00:27.970
abstract questions.

00:00:27.970 --> 00:00:33.700
The more pressing one is if internet citizenship
exists, then are there some things that we

00:00:33.700 --> 00:00:36.579
could be doing to be better internet citizens?

00:00:36.579 --> 00:00:37.880
I think yes.

00:00:37.880 --> 00:00:40.610
And here is my first crack at some guidelines.

00:00:40.610 --> 00:00:42.540
People on the internet are people.

00:00:42.540 --> 00:00:44.490
The internet is real life.

00:00:44.490 --> 00:00:47.180
Treat people like people online or off.

00:00:47.180 --> 00:00:49.820
Every person is complicated and impossible
to fully understand.

00:00:49.820 --> 00:00:54.710
If you think you've got somebody pegged completely
after reading a 25 word comment, you're wrong.

00:00:54.710 --> 00:00:59.770
Removing comments and communities that dehumanize
or mock other people is not censorship, it's

00:00:59.770 --> 00:01:00.770
kindness.

00:01:00.770 --> 00:01:03.719
Everybody can see and read the stuff that
you put on the internet, and it will probably

00:01:03.719 --> 00:01:06.750
be there pretty much forever, so be thoughtful.

00:01:06.750 --> 00:01:10.390
BS detection is one of the most crucial skills
of the online citizen, and it takes a long

00:01:10.390 --> 00:01:12.010
time to develop that set of skills.

00:01:12.010 --> 00:01:15.040
In fact, it's something that I think should
be taught in schools everywhere.

00:01:15.040 --> 00:01:16.240
The number one rule, though?

00:01:16.240 --> 00:01:19.570
Cite your sources and don't share statistics
that you can't find sources for.

00:01:19.570 --> 00:01:22.299
Just because something seems like it's true
does not mean it is.

00:01:22.299 --> 00:01:26.490
And in general, don't share things that might
be lies, even if they help you make a point

00:01:26.490 --> 00:01:28.159
that is really important to make.

00:01:28.159 --> 00:01:31.280
Mark Twain said it best, of course, "A lie
can travel halfway round the world while the

00:01:31.280 --> 00:01:32.800
truth is putting on its shoes.

00:01:32.800 --> 00:01:35.960
Don't be part of the problem, help the truth
put its shoes on."

00:01:35.960 --> 00:01:38.820
You don't know everything.

00:01:38.820 --> 00:01:40.010
Nobody does.

00:01:40.010 --> 00:01:42.500
Nobody even knows most things.

00:01:42.500 --> 00:01:46.659
Don't steal stuff from creators and then repost
it without attribution.

00:01:46.659 --> 00:01:51.290
Do, however, attempt to source unsourced content
by using reverse image search engines like

00:01:51.290 --> 00:01:52.290
TinEye.

00:01:52.290 --> 00:01:55.830
Not only is this good internet citizenship,
it's also a great way to hone your research

00:01:55.830 --> 00:01:56.830
skills.

00:01:56.830 --> 00:01:58.409
Anyone can edit Wikipedia, for real.

00:01:58.409 --> 00:02:01.521
If you see a grammatical or spelling mistake,
go ahead and fix it.

00:02:01.521 --> 00:02:04.700
If you find a vandalized page, you can fix
that by going to the page history, finding

00:02:04.700 --> 00:02:07.159
the change that introduced the vandalism,
and then clicking undo.

00:02:07.159 --> 00:02:08.159
It's that simple.

00:02:08.159 --> 00:02:12.540
If you'd like to do more than just fix small
errors and vandalism on Wikipedia articles,

00:02:12.540 --> 00:02:16.310
you can read more about the philosophy and
strategies of the serious Wikipedia editor

00:02:16.310 --> 00:02:18.900
before you start to actually make big changes.

00:02:18.900 --> 00:02:21.980
And in general, if you're joining any new
online community of people doing something

00:02:21.980 --> 00:02:25.700
together or enjoying something together, recognize
that there are probably going to be some rules

00:02:25.700 --> 00:02:28.940
that you do not yet know, and that you're
probably going to make some early mistakes.

00:02:28.940 --> 00:02:32.700
And if you are part of one of those existing
communities and new people come in, expect

00:02:32.700 --> 00:02:36.459
that they're not gonna know all the rules,
and they are gonna make some mistakes.

00:02:36.459 --> 00:02:40.580
And finally, don't expect that everyone or
even you is going to be able to follow all

00:02:40.580 --> 00:02:42.480
of these rules all of the time.

00:02:42.480 --> 00:02:43.880
Sometimes, we get angry.

00:02:43.880 --> 00:02:45.540
Sometimes, we all act like jerks.

00:02:45.540 --> 00:02:49.400
Be understanding of yourself and be understanding
of others.

00:02:49.400 --> 00:02:52.810
When I asked Twitter and Tumblr what the most
important rules of internet citizenship were,

00:02:52.810 --> 00:02:56.770
a lot of people pointed me toward Wheaton's
Law, which I like, but is a little bit subjective.

00:02:56.770 --> 00:03:00.150
Reworded slightly for the families out there,
Wheaton's Law is basically just "Don't be

00:03:00.150 --> 00:03:01.150
a jerk."

00:03:01.150 --> 00:03:03.140
But the response I liked most went further
than that.

00:03:03.140 --> 00:03:06.519
"Always leave people and places better than
the way that you found them."

00:03:06.519 --> 00:03:10.340
That's what we should be working for in this
wonderful place that has, in many ways, become

00:03:10.340 --> 00:03:11.340
my home.

00:03:11.340 --> 00:03:13.080
John, I'll see you on Tuesday.

